  "final_message": "I wrote `answer.json` in the repository root. Three functions would have to change:\n\n- **`usage.report_usage`**: it stores the client's millisecond `timestamp` as given. It also defaults to `to_millis(ctx.clock.now())`. It would need to convert the incoming milliseconds to seconds, and default to `ctx.clock.now()`.\n- **`usage.records_in_period`**: it converts the period bounds with `to_millis` to compare against stored timestamps. Stored values would now be seconds, so it would compare against the period bounds directly.\n- **`api.report_usage`**: it returns `record[\"timestamp\"]` in the response. That would now be seconds, so it would need to convert back to milliseconds. The existing test expects the API to echo milliseconds.\n\nI left some functions off the list on purpose:\n\n- `engine.Engine.report_usage` only passes the value through. Only its docstring mentions milliseconds.\n- `usage.aggregate_usage`, `usage.usage_summary` and `invoices.subscription_lines` work with period seconds and call `records_in_period`.\n- `store.Store.usage_for` doesn't read the timestamp.\n\nI made no other file changes. I did move `answer.json` once by mistake and then put it back in the repo root.",
